<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel José Arce</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1787">1787</a>–<a href="/years/1847">1847</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel José Arce (1787–1847) was a Salvadoran soldier and politician who became the first president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a> in 1825. Elected after a disputed vote in congress, he drifted towards the Guatemalan conservatives and in 1826 had the liberal Guatemalan head of state <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-barrundia">Juan Barrundia</a> arrested, starting a civil war. The liberal victory under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> in 1829 ended his presidency and sent him into exile.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-jose-arce.jpg" alt="Manuel José Arce" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_Jos%C3%A9_Arce_y_Fagoaga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel José Arce y Fagoaga was born on 1 January 1787 in the Intendancy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, son of the Spaniard Bernardo José de Arce, its colonial intendant in 1800 and 1801, and Antonia Fagoaga. Sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> in 1801, he graduated in philosophy from the Colegio de San Francisco Borja and began medicine at the Universidad de San Carlos de Borromeo, but broke off when his father fell ill. In December 1808 he married Felipa de Aranzamendi y Aguiar in San Salvador.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Arce joined the first Cry for Independence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> on 5 November 1811, led by his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-matias-delgado">José Matías Delgado</a>, the vicar of San Salvador; the rebels held the government for nearly a month. His part in the second rising of 22 January 1814 cost him four years in prison. He opposed the Mexican Empire of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-de-iturbide">Agustín de Iturbide</a>, and as a colonel he was one of the commanders of San Salvador when Manuel Arzú's Guatemalan troops, fighting for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, entered the city on 3 June 1822 after nine hours of fighting. On a journey back from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> in 1823 he tried in Mexico to raise a force to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, without success.</p><p>Elected in October 1823 to the executive triumvirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, he served from 15 March to 20 October 1824, when the government pacified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a> with little violence. In the 1825 elections <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-cecilio-del-valle">José Cecilio del Valle</a> won the most votes, but the Liberal Congress ruled that he lacked an absolute majority and chose Arce, who served from 29 April 1825 to 13 April 1829.</p><p>He soon lost the support of the Liberals in Congress and leaned on the clergy and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conservative-party-uk">Conservative Party</a>. His removal of the Liberal Guatemalan governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-barrundia">Juan Barrundia</a> led the Salvadoran state to rebel. When he tried to resume the presidency from Vice President Mariano Beltranena y Llano, Beltranena refused and held office until liberal troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a> in April 1829.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1832 Arce led an expedition from Soconusco against the federal government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> and was defeated on 24 February 1832. Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-salvador">El Salvador</a> in 1842, he soon fled again, and in 1844 he led armed attempts against Francisco Malespín. He left politics in 1846 to write a book on the reorganisation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, and died in poverty in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> on 14 December 1847. His remains were moved to the Military Museum of the Armed Forces of El Salvador in 2003, and the town of El Chilamatal was renamed Ciudad Arce in 1947.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel José Arce” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Manuel José Arce</b> of El Salvador became the first federal president in 1825, after a disputed vote in congress.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=13.7,-89.19&amp;year=1826" aria-label="San Salvador on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Manuel José Arce"><image href="/maps/globes/g27000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5067 3840h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5067 3840h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=13.7,-89.19&amp;year=1826">What was here in 1826? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
